    "question": "can a data-movement schedule resolve the A/B exchange at build time, so that the emitted circuit is smaller rather than the executed one?",
    "why_it_matters": "a saving from a *read* needs `mr`; a saving from a *schedule* is in the emitted circuit, so it survives the pinned competition binary (see Experiment D)",
    "C1_rebased": {
      "change": "do not exchange (n_A, n_a, reg_a) with (n_B, n_b, reg_b); reassign which physical register plays the A role in each round from the round index alone, and hand the Forney block the register holding role B",
      "assumption": "the sub-step counter advances once per live round, so `c3` fires in rounds with i % 4 == 3 and the role assignment is a function of the round index",
      "predicted_saving_per_shot": 64224.0,
      "why_exactly": "the arm removes the region, so the saving is the region's whole executed cost, including the two control ANDs inside it",
      "predicted_correct_if_and_only_if": "the c3-round set is the same for every shot",
      "falsifier": "this arm itself: if it is incorrect, the schedule is data-dependent and no build-time resolution exists"
    },
    "C2_pattern": {
      "method": "read the c3 control into a per-round qubit of a probe arm and compare the patterns across the 64 shots",
      "prediction": "the pattern is a function of the shot's syndrome, so distinct patterns > 1 and C1 is unavailable; one pattern would make C1 exact"
    },
    "C3_flag_redundant": {
